  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "An array AAA is called weird if it can be sorted in non-decreasing order by applying the given operation any number of times:\n\n\nSelect any index iii (1≤i≤∣A∣)(1 \\le i \\le |A|)(1≤i≤∣A∣) and set Ai:=−AiA_i := -A_iAi​:=−Ai​.\nFor example: A=[2,1,3]A = [2, 1, 3]A=[2,1,3] is weird since after applying the operation at i=1i = 1i=1, AAA becomes [−2,1,3][-2, 1, 3][−2,1,3] which is sorted.\n\nJJ has a permutation PPP of length NNN. He wants to find the number of subarrays of PPP which are weird. Can you help him?\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line contains a single integer TTT — the number of test cases. Then the test cases follow.\nThe first line of each test case contains an integer NNN — the size of the permutation PPP.\nThe second line of each test case contains NNN space-separated integers P1,P2,…,PNP_1, P_2, \\dots, P_NP1​,P2​,…,PN​ denoting the permutation PPP.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the number of weird subarrays of PPP.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤N≤1051 \\leq N \\leq 10^51≤N≤105",
      "PPP is a permutation.",
      "Sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 2⋅1052 \\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n3\n2 3 1\n3\n2 1 3\n4\n1 2 3 4",
        "output": "5\n6\n10",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest Case 1: Weird subarrays of PPP are: [2],[3],[1],[2,3],[3,1][2], [3], [1], [2, 3], [3, 1][2],[3],[1],[2,3],[3,1].\nTest Case 2: Weird subarrays of PPP are: [2],[1],[3],[2,1],[1,3],[2,1,3][2], [1], [3], [2, 1], [1, 3], [2, 1, 3][2],[1],[3],[2,1],[1,3],[2,1,3]."
      }
    ]
